<!--
  Rendered by templatefile() from apis.tf, once per API. The validate-jwt block appears only where
  entra_tenant_id is set.

  WHY IT IS CONDITIONAL. auth.key-source decides who issued the token the service will accept, and
  it is per-environment. Under SELF_ISSUED the service signs tokens with a key from its own vault
  and carries its own issuer - Microsoft has never heard of them, so an unconditional validate-jwt
  would reject every single one. The workflow applies whatever apim/*.tfvars exists, so the day a
  SELF_ISSUED environment gets its own tfvars this has to already be off for it. Leave
  entra_tenant_id unset there and the gateway falls back to throughput protection alone.

  WHY THE AUDIENCE IS PER-API. One deployment serves a sandbox for every registered API, and
  `roles` cannot tell them apart: the role is `app.read` on all of them, by design, because a
  consumer evaluating several APIs should not have to learn a different role name for each. The
  audience is therefore the whole access boundary, and it is checked here before `roles` is read.
  entra_audience comes from the per-API block in the tfvars, never from a shared value - a single
  gateway-wide audience would make a token minted for one sandbox valid against all of them, which
  is exactly the hole the per-API resource apps were created to close.

  WHY /scenarios IS EXEMPT. It is the discovery endpoint. A consumer reads authentication.mode
  from it to find out which realm they are in, so it has to answer before they hold anything at
  all - a 401 there makes the sandbox impossible to start using. The service exempts the same path
  (AuthorizationPolicy.isScenarioListing), so this is not the gateway being more permissive than
  the backend; the two agree. The exemption is an allow-list of one on UrlTemplate rather than a
  deny-list, so an operation added later is protected by default rather than open by accident.
  UrlTemplate and not Operation.Id: path templates survive the import verbatim, operation ids are
  APIM's to derive.

  WHY THE THROUGHPUT KEYS NAME THE API. counter_key is "try-<short code>", so each sandbox gets
  its own bucket. Keying on the subscription alone would be wrong twice over: the products are
  per-API so a key belongs to one sandbox anyway, and a shared counter would let traffic to one
  API exhaust another's allowance. The IP fallback is for the unsubscribed case; it is coarse, and
  it is meant to be.
-->
<policies>
    <inbound>
%{ if entra_tenant_id != null ~}
        <choose>
            <when condition="@(context.Operation.UrlTemplate == &quot;/scenarios&quot;)" />
            <when condition="@(context.Request.Headers.ContainsKey(&quot;Authorization&quot;))">
                <validate-jwt header-name="Authorization" failed-validation-httpcode="401" failed-validation-error-message="Your JWT token failed CNP validation! ">
                    <openid-config url="https://login.microsoftonline.com/${entra_tenant_id}/v2.0/.well-known/openid-configuration" />
                    <audiences>
                        <audience>${entra_audience}</audience>
                    </audiences>
                    <issuers>
                        <issuer>https://login.microsoftonline.com/${entra_tenant_id}/v2.0</issuer>
                    </issuers>
                    <required-claims>
                        <claim name="aud" match="any">
                            <value>${entra_audience}</value>
                        </claim>
                        <claim name="roles" match="any">
%{ for role in required_roles ~}
                            <value>${role}</value>
%{ endfor ~}
                        </claim>
                    </required-claims>
                </validate-jwt>
            </when>
            <otherwise>
                <return-response>
                    <set-status code="401" reason="Unauthorized" />
                    <set-header name="Content-Type" exists-action="override">
                        <value>application/json</value>
                    </set-header>
                    <set-body>{"error":"Missing CNP Authorization header."}</set-body>
                </return-response>
            </otherwise>
        </choose>
%{ endif ~}
        <rate-limit-by-key calls="30" renewal-period="60" counter-key="@((context.Subscription?.Key ?? context.Request.IpAddress) + &quot;:${counter_key}&quot;)" />
        <quota-by-key calls="500" renewal-period="86400" counter-key="@((context.Subscription?.Key ?? context.Request.IpAddress) + &quot;:${counter_key}&quot;)" />
        <base />
    </inbound>
    <backend>
        <limit-concurrency key="@((context.Subscription?.Key ?? context.Request.IpAddress) + &quot;:${counter_key}&quot;)" max-count="10">
            <forward-request />
        </limit-concurrency>
    </backend>
    <outbound>
        <base />
    </outbound>
    <on-error>
        <base />
    </on-error>
</policies>
